# Line endings, declared rather than inferred.
#
# core.autocrlf=true was converting on the way in and back on the way out, so
# every checkout produced "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings and left the
# working tree byte-different from what was stored. Git had to guess per file;
# a checkout on Linux would have flipped the scripts the other way.
#
# The rule below is the one the toolchains actually want:
#   * text formats are stored and checked out as LF — the Go and Dart toolchains
#     are happiest with it, and it keeps diffs free of whole-file churn
#   * Windows scripts keep CRLF, because PowerShell and cmd parse LF-only
#     .ps1/.cmd files badly enough to produce confusing errors
#
# With these rules set, the warnings stop and a fresh clone on any platform
# produces the same bytes.

* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell and batch scripts are the one deliberate exception.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.gif binary

# Test certificates for the CONNECT-proxy test. PEM is text, but `eol=lf`
# rewriting it makes OpenSSL's parser complain about the base64 body, and a
# fixture that fails to load turns the proxy test into a false pass.
*.pem binary
*.webp binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.so binary
*.dat binary
*.db binary
*.dylib binary
*.pdb binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
